1. What is ETL and ELT? Explain with examples.

✅ ETL = Extract → Transform → Load

You clean and transform the data before loading into the warehouse.

Example (Traditional DW):

Extract from SQL Server

Transform in Informatica

Load into Teradata


✅ ELT = Extract → Load → Transform

You load raw data first, then transform using the cloud engine.

Example (Azure):

Extract from source

Load to ADLS (Bronze)

Transform in Databricks / Synapse (Silver → Gold)


✅ When to use ETL?

When transformations are heavy and need specialized tools (Informatica, SSIS)

On-prem systems

When data volumes are moderate


✅ When to use ELT?

Cloud setups

Big data workloads

When using Spark / Synapse SQL



---

2. What is a Data Lake? What are its layers (Bronze/Silver/Gold)?

✅ Data Lake = Storage repository for raw, semi-processed, and curated data.

✅ Bronze Layer (Raw)

Stores uncleaned raw data exactly as received

Formats: JSON, CSV, Parquet, Delta

No transformations applied

Good for audits & replay


✅ Silver Layer (Cleaned)

Data is cleaned

Deduplicated

Enriched with lookups

Normalized schema

Partitioned


✅ Gold Layer (Business)

Final business-ready tables

Aggregations

Facts & dimensions

Optimized for reporting (Power BI, Synapse)



---

3. What is a Delta Table? Why is Delta better than Parquet?

✅ Delta = Parquet + Transaction Log

Delta Lake provides:

✅ ACID transactions
✅ Time travel
✅ Upserts (MERGE)
✅ Faster reads/writes
✅ Schema evolution
✅ Data reliability

✅ Parquet vs Delta

Feature	Parquet	Delta

Storage Format	Columnar	Columnar + Log
ACID	❌ No	✅ Yes
MERGE/UPSERT	❌ No	✅ Yes
Time Travel	❌ No	✅ Yes
Schema Evolution	❌ Limited	✅ Full



---

4. What is Partitioning in Spark?

Partitioning means splitting data into folders based on a column value.

Example:

/sales/year=2023/month=01/
/sales/year=2023/month=02/

✅ Why use it?

Faster filtering

Less data scanned

Better performance


✅ When NOT to partition?

Too many partitions

High-cardinality columns (e.g., customer_id)



---

5. What is Bucketing in Spark?

Bucketing divides data into a fixed number of files using hashing.

df.write.bucketBy(8, "customer_id").saveAsTable("bucketed_customers")

✅ Why use bucketing?

Faster JOIN performance

Better clustering

Lower shuffle cost



